	{
		"title": "readelf -a, and what a clean run means",
		"kind": "text",
		"command": "readelf -a interop 2>&1 | grep -i -e warning -e error",
		"request": "The finished executable, handed to readelf's everything-mode: the ELF header, the section headers, the program headers, the section-to-segment mapping, the symbol table and any relocations left in the file",
		"request_hex": "",
		"response": "Exit status 0, nothing at all on stderr, and not one line of the dump containing 'warning', 'error', 'corrupt' or 'unable to'",
		"response_hex": "",
		"note": "sh_link on .symtab, sh_info on .symtab, sh_entsize on anything with fixed-size entries: get one of those wrong and the program still runs, readelf still prints a table, and one small warning line is the only thing that ever tells you.",
